## Credentials & trust

### [V23-58](change.log#L486) — the hub published its own cluster credentials, and the repo told it to

These are credentials, not configuration:

### [V23-59](change.log#L584) — a follower could not bootstrap without permanently weakening its validation

Surfaced by the chain reset: after `reset-follower-to-genesis.sh` this node sat at `{"detail":"no blocks yet"}` and could not reach height 1, with a correct `genesis.json` already installed at `/var/lib/aitbc/data/<chain>/genesis.json`.

### [V23-60](change.log#L640) — RPC genesis bootstrap could never reach an HTTPS hub

Found in the operator's `journalctl` output while confirming V23-59 had worked:

### [V23-68](change.log#L1151) — a key named for a coordinator, published as if it were a faucet token

The hub publishes `COORDINATOR_API_KEY` in `https://hub.aitbc.bubuit.net/agent/blockchain.env`, a file it serves to anyone (200, 1215 bytes). The intent is sound — an island needs to call `/register` and `/execute` without an operator handing out credentials — but that key is not scoped to those two endpoints:

### [V23-68b](change.log#L4147) — the fix for a published credential, and the two ways it could not have worked · CONFIRMED → RESOLVED

V23-68 removed the promotion that made `COORDINATOR_API_KEY` — a key published in a world-readable bootstrap file — a miner credential whenever no miner keys were configured, and told operators to set `MINER_API_KEYS` instead. Both halves of that were unreachable, and main was left red.

### [V23-68c](change.log#L4235) — the published key was the miner's key, and the system said "invalid" instead · CONFIRMED → RESOLVED

V23-68 removed the code path that promoted `COORDINATOR_API_KEY` to a miner credential when `miner_api_keys` was empty. V23-68b made the replacement setting readable. This entry is what setting it on the deployed host actually found, which is worse than either.


## Database / Alembic

### [V23-53](change.log#L215) — an idempotency fix that only works on one of the two backends

`6a2413138` made two migrations re-runnable, because a deploy had to be run with `--no-migrate` to get past them. The coordinator-api half is fine. The governance half rewrote `002_v073_onchain_governance_fields` from Alembic operations into raw SQL:

### [V23-64](change.log#L922) — the account canonicalisation is right; it dropped escrow's foreign keys on the way

`bee3e4a83` closed the latent half of V23-63: an `AccountAddress` column type that normalises `0x…`, `ait1…` and `aitbc1…` to one lowercase `ait1` form on both bind and result, plus the same normalisation on trie keys. That is the right shape of fix — one normalisation point on account access rather than patches at each call site — and it works.

### [V23-65](change.log#L969) — the account normalisation rewrites two fields that a signature covers

The chain reset cleared the one `aitbc1` row that had already tripped this. It did not remove the cause, and the cause is reachable from outside.

### [V23-65 (fix)](change.log#L1011) — signed columns store what was signed

The two `xfail(strict=True)` tests above are now passing tests.

### [V23-75](change.log#L1790) — `alembic upgrade head` built two of trading's nine tables · CONFIRMED → RESOLVED

`apps/trading` manages its schema with Alembic and means it: `storage.init_db()` creates nothing, with a comment saying so — *"Schema management is Alembic's job. Do not call create_all() here. Run `alembic upgrade head` before service startup."* That is the right policy.


## Coin requests & accounts

### [V23-62](change.log#L728) — the faucet's treasury endpoint had no tests, and the deleted ones were green

Two findings, from following up the `test(cleanup)` sweep.

### [V23-62 (fix)](change.log#L785) — the execute endpoint now consults the request it claims to execute

The four `xfail(strict=True)` tests added above are now passing tests. `request_id` had been reaching the handler and going nowhere but a log line; it is now the only field that matters.

### [V23-62 (registration)](change.log#L839) — how a follower's request gets a row at the hub

The execute fix refuses a `request_id` the hub has never seen, which is correct and broke a real flow: the hub's own database showed `req-follower-1782118019`, so requests raised on follower islands do reach it. Those requests exist only in the island's database, so the hub has nothing to consult.

### [V23-66](change.log#L1048) — the coin-request database claims a payout the chain has never heard of

`req-follower-1782118019` records a `transaction_hash` for a transaction that no longer exists. The hub's chain was reset on 2026-08-15 and rebuilt from genesis; the coin-request database was not, and nothing connects them. So it goes on asserting that a payout settled, on a chain nobody has any more.

### [V23-67](change.log#L1110) — one grant per agent turned out to mean one grant per *name*

`req-follower-1782118019` was granted 100 AIT to `ait1c10f…`. `req-follower-1782118019-v2` was granted 100 AIT to the same `ait1c10f…` and auto-approved. Reported from the hub, with the cause: the second request declared `sender="follower-ait-reset"` where the first declared `sender="follower"`.


## Test suite cleanup

### [V23-61](change.log#L689) — two passing test files were deleted by the red-CI cleanup

While diagnosing the red gates on `main`, I traced the four remaining local failures to three root causes, none of which was the event-loop misconfiguration this log previously blamed:

### [V23-69](change.log#L1195) — fourteen of sixteen app test suites had never run

V23-67 turned up `apps/agent-coordinator/tests` sitting outside `testpaths`. It was not a one-off: `testpaths` listed two app suites out of sixteen, leaving **58 test files** that no run has ever collected — including 112 tests for coordinator-api, a deployed service, and the suites for wallet, exchange, marketplace, trading, gpu and pool-hub.

### [V23-70](change.log#L1241) — the last three app suites, and a guard for the failure that hid them

V23-69 left three suites out because they did not collect: `blockchain-explorer` (1 error), `miner` (3), `zk-circuits` (3). The errors were `No module named 'main'`, `'production_miner'` and `'zk_cache'` — one shared cause.

### [V23-71](change.log#L1284) — 184 test files were deleted for producing no results

Five commits dated 2026-08-14 — `0edfb53b7` through `ab846475c`, the last titled "remove skipped, errored, and failed test functions" — deleted **184 test files, about 51,000 lines**.

### [V23-71b](change.log#L1345) — the app tranche, and what the deletions were actually made of

49 more deleted test files across eight apps. **41 restored, 11 held back**, and the split is the useful part: the 2026-08-14 sweep was not uniformly wrong, and it was not uniformly right.

### [V23-72](change.log#L1409) — two services, one table registry, four workarounds

Every SQLModel class in this repo registers its `Table` on one process-global `MetaData` unless it says otherwise. `apps/governance` and `apps/coordinator-api` both model governance and both name their tables `governance_profiles`, `proposals`, `votes`, `dao_treasury` and `transparency_reports`.

### [V23-73](change.log#L1492) — the test suite was running against the deployed databases

Adding `apps/governance/tests` to `testpaths`, which V23-72 was supposed to make possible, broke five gpu and trading tests instead. Governance's conftest assigns `os.environ["DATABASE_URL"]` at import — correctly, and for a reason its own docstring explains — but the assignment is process-global and never restored, and gpu and trading both read `DATABASE_URL` at import time to build their engines.

### [V23-74](change.log#L1560) — the chain shared its table registry with the coordinator, and two hooks paid for it

Every SQLModel class in the repo registers its `Table` on one process-global `MetaData` unless it says otherwise. The chain is not the only thing here with a `Transaction`: `coordinator-api` has one too, along with its own `Block` and `Receipt`.

### [V23-71c](change.log#L1613) — the seven files the shared registry had made unrestorable

V23-71 restored what it could and stopped at seven files that failed in a full run while passing on their own. Six were coordinator-api files erroring on `no such table: users`, and the cause was not in them: `apps/blockchain-node/tests/conftest.py` and `tests/integration/conftest.py` were emptying SQLModel's global registry out from under every suite collected after them.

### [V23-71 tranche 3](change.log#L1662) — the 103 deleted root tests, and why `tests/` was never collected

The cleanup commits removed 103 files under `tests/`. Ten had already come back; of the remaining 93, **90 are restored and three stay deleted**. Of the 90, **45 sit in directories `testpaths` already names, and all 45 pass unchanged — 722 tests.** That is the whole story for half the tranche: they were deleted as "skipped, errored, and failed" and they are none of those.

### [V23-84](change.log#L2824) — 87 tests, collected by a fallback, run by nobody · CONFIRMED → RESOLVED

`cli/tests` was the last suite in the repository that no run collected. It is 87 tests over seven files, and the root suite went from 6,469 to **6,556** by adding one line to `testpaths`.

### [V23-93](change.log#L4457) — a gate on seven production-writing scripts was skipping 159 unit tests · CONFIRMED → RESOLVED

`tests/verification/` holds seven modules that are not unit tests: they read the live hub's head and then POST newly built blocks to `/rpc/importBlock`, jobs to `/v1/jobs` and miner results to `/v1/miners/{id}/result`. A passing run means blocks were accepted into production.


## API correctness

### [V23-76](change.log#L1874) — `GET /offers/{id}` told clients a missing offer existed · CONFIRMED → RESOLVED

`GET /v1/marketplace/offers/{offer_id}` answered **200 with a body of `null`** for an offer that is not there. The route called `MarketplaceService.get_offer`, which returns `None` when nothing matches, and returned it. FastAPI serialized `None` to `null` and sent it with the default status.

### [V23-80](change.log#L2294) — 703 operations, no 404s, and a drift guard nobody had run · CONFIRMED → RESOLVED

Filed out of V23-76, which fixed `GET /v1/marketplace/offers/{id}` answering 200-null for a missing offer and noted that the published spec documented `200` and `422` for all eight `/offers/{...}` operations — including the six that had always returned 404.

### [V23-81](change.log#L2405) — a 500, a 200 that wrote an orphan, and a zero that meant nothing · CONFIRMED → RESOLVED

Filed out of V23-80's "noticed and not fixed": three marketplace routes under the same prefixes as V23-76's never answered 404. Probed against a throwaway database before touching anything, because **the filed description was wrong about one of the three**:

### [V23-82](change.log#L2533) — the drift guard nothing invoked, and the reason it could not have failed anyway · CONFIRMED → RESOLVED

`make openapi-check` has existed since v0.22 and works. Nothing ran it. It was therefore red on main for most of v0.23 without anyone hearing about it — agent-coordinator missing a route, blockchain-node missing a route, and coordinator-api's money fields still published as `"type": "number"` after the Decimal work, so a client generated from the spec would have parsed a decimal string as a float.

### [V23-83](change.log#L2673) — two vocabularies for one column, and the endpoint that could not survive either · CONFIRMED → RESOLVED

`POST /v1/marketplace/offers/{id}/cancel` answered **500 for every offer in the deployed database**. Not most, not the ones in an odd state — every one, by two different routes to the same failure.

### [V23-86](change.log#L3166) — a workstation path that outlived the workstation, in 24 tracked scripts · CONFIRMED → RESOLVED

`/home/oib/windsurf/aitbc` is a checkout on a developer's old machine. The repo is at `/opt/aitbc`. 24 tracked `*.py`/`*.sh` files still name that path, and every one of them either fails at its first command or operates on nothing.

### [V23-87](change.log#L3321) — a security fix that flipped one keyword, and the script that stopped at its first command · CONFIRMED → RESOLVED

`scripts/testing/qa-cycle.py` fetches `main`, runs the two package test suites, lints, requests sibling reviews on open PRs and reports repository status. Every caller of its `run_cmd` passed a single string:

### [V23-88](change.log#L3476) — a lint fix that deleted nine defaults, and the five tests that recorded the result · CONFIRMED → RESOLVED

Nine query parameters across five marketplace endpoints were required. Asking for analytics without saying which period, or listing transactions without a filter, answered `422` — and the published spec agreed, because FastAPI had been told they were mandatory:

### [V23-89](change.log#L3664) — 283 query parameters that could never be what their type said they were · CONFIRMED → RESOLVED

V23-88 fixed nine query parameters in `apps/marketplace` that FastAPI treated as required because they were annotated `X | None` with no default. This is the same fault everywhere else: **283 parameters, across 121 handlers in 30 files, in six applications.**

### [V23-90](change.log#L3777) — a diverged follower reported health four different ways for 46 hours · CONFIRMED → RESOLVED

The hub's chain database was reset to genesis on 2026-08-15 at 11:12 local. The follower on `aitbc3` was at height 4,211 of the chain the hub had just discarded — its last import was block 4,211 at 11:12:01, and the hub's replacement block 1 is stamped 11:12:19.

### [V23-91](change.log#L3952) — a ZK subsystem that reported four circuits and could not produce one proof · CONFIRMED → RESOLVED

`ZKProofService` loaded, logged `Available circuits: [...]`, and answered `enabled: true`. Every proof it was asked for failed, and every failure was reported as a proof failure rather than as a missing dependency. Five faults stacked here, each sufficient on its own to make proving impossible.


## Lint, type checking & guards

### [V23-78](change.log#L2020) — a log format switch that has not been read since June · CONFIRMED → RESOLVED

`setup_logger` and `configure_logging` both took a `structured: bool` they never read; `setup_logger` also took a `format_string` it never read. That is the reported finding, and it is only the near end of it. The same switch had a third face — a `LOG_FORMAT` environment variable, read by `_get_log_format()`, which had no callers — and *that* one was wired into production: `LOG_FORMAT=json` was set in **five systemd units** and asserted by **two release gates**, one of which fails the release if it is missing.

### [V23-85](change.log#L2967) — the security gate that exited on a usage error, in a hook nothing installed · CONFIRMED → RESOLVED

Two independent failures, either of which alone would have been enough. The filed finding was the second one.

### [V23-94](change.log#L4537) — the ML circuits' learning-rate checks did the opposite of what their comments said · CONFIRMED → RESOLVED

Two circuits back `POST /v1/ml-zk/prove/training` and `POST /v1/ml-zk/prove/modular`. Both claimed to constrain the learning rate to `0 < lr < 1`. Neither did, in three different ways, and every one of them was invisible because nothing in the repository could compile a circuit.

### [V23-95](change.log#L4760) — the build tree kept the unsatisfiable circuits V23-94 fixed only in the service tree · CONFIRMED → RESOLVED

`ml_training_verification` and `modular_ml_components` exist twice. V23-94 fixed the copies under `apps/coordinator-api/src/coordinator_api/contexts/zk_applications/zk-circuits/`, because those are the ones `ZKProofService` resolves, and documented the other copies as broken in `apps/zk-circuits/README.md` rather than fixing them.


## Service health & monitoring

### [V23-92](change.log#L4351) — a shop node notifying a service it is correct not to run · CONFIRMED → RESOLVED

`aitbc coin-requests approve` and `reject` finish by calling `send_agent_notification()` to tell the requesting agent what happened. On this host — a follower/shop node — that call could not succeed, for two independent reasons, and both were invisible because the function catches everything and prints.

### [V23-96](change.log#L4882) — Pool Hub's health endpoint answered 404, and 500'd on the outage it exists to report · CONFIRMED → RESOLVED

Three defects sat in one 54-line handler, and a fourth aimed every operator tool at the wrong service. Together they meant that no path through the system could answer "is Pool Hub healthy?" — the endpoint was unreachable at its conventional address, the CLI asked a different service entirely, and when something genuinely broke the endpoint crashed rather than saying so.

### [V23-97](change.log#L5044) — Pool Hub's billing sync and resolved-violation query never built a statement · CONFIRMED → RESOLVED

Three query filters in Pool Hub spelled the SQLAlchemy operator `isnot_`. There is no such operator. A column offers `is_`, `is_not`, and the legacy alias `isnot`; the trailing underscore matches nothing, so `InstrumentedAttribute.__getattr__` raised `AttributeError` while the statement was still being *constructed* — before any database was touched.

### [V23-98](change.log#L5144) — the health monitor watched five of the eleven services running on this host · CONFIRMED → RESOLVED

V23-96 added Pool Hub to `SERVICE_ENDPOINTS` after a 404 on its `/health` survived in production because nothing ever probed it. Pool Hub was not the exception. This host runs eleven AITBC services; the map watched five of them, plus two units that are not installed here at all.


## Orphan modules

### [V23-103](change.log#L5268) — 22 modules nothing imports, and nothing that would have noticed a 23rd · CONFIRMED → GUARDED, DELETIONS PENDING

V23-102 deleted `MarketplaceMonitor`: 280 lines of marketplace alerting no code path could reach, holding a module-level singleton whose `start()` nobody called. That fixed one module. It did not touch the reason there were more behind it, which is that nothing in this repository has ever noticed when a file stops being imported.
